---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/intermediate/public-health-and-epidemiology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Public health is <strong>medicine at population scale</strong>: instead of treating one
    patient, it shifts the whole distribution of risk. Epidemiology is its measurement
    discipline — and its interventions (sanitation, vaccination, tobacco control) have saved
    more lives than all clinical medicine combined. COVID made the vocabulary (R₀, flattening
    the curve, excess deaths) household property.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the population lens, its measures, and its greatest wins" widget="equationStep" config={epiCfg} />

  <h2>The subtleties that matter</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Correlation at population level still needs causal care:</strong> confounding is worse, not better, in big data. The causal-inference lesson (statistics ladder) is this field's daily toolkit — and its natural experiments are elegant.</li>
    <li><strong>Screening has costs:</strong> testing everyone finds pseudo-diseases and false positives — overdiagnosis debates (prostate, breast screening) are quantitative ethics, not bureaucracy-bashing.</li>
    <li><strong>Health inequities are measurable:</strong> life expectancy can differ 20 years between neighbourhoods a few miles apart. Public health treats the distribution, not just the average.</li>
    <li><strong>The pandemic taught the vocabulary:</strong> excess mortality (the most comparable metric), case fatality vs infection fatality rates, and why testing capacity shapes the apparent curve. Epidemiological literacy is now civic infrastructure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Snow on cholera (1855); Rothman, Epidemiology: An Introduction; WHO World Health Statistics; CDC achievements in public health series.</p>
</TopicPage>
